   ['The planned (official) curriculum is set out in policy documents, syllabuses and textbooks. The taught curriculum is what teachers actually do; the learned curriculum is what pupils actually learn. The hidden curriculum is learned without being taught on purpose, such as punctuality, gender roles or respect for authority, through rules, routines and relationships. The null curriculum is what is left out, which also sends a message.',
    'Curricula can be subject-centred (organised by traditional disciplines), learner-centred (built on learners\' interests and needs) or problem- and competency-centred (organised around real-life problems and competencies, as in Kenya\'s Competency-Based Curriculum). Jerome Bruner\'s spiral curriculum revisits key ideas at increasing depth across the years.',
    'Ralph Tyler\'s Basic Principles of Curriculum and Instruction (1949) proposed a rational model: define objectives, select learning experiences, organise them, and evaluate whether objectives were achieved. Later models, such as Wheeler\'s cyclical model and Taba\'s grassroots model, stress continuous revision and teacher involvement. Curriculum change also needs teacher training, materials and assessment reform, or it stays on paper.'],
   ['Planned → taught → learned', 'Hidden curriculum: unintended lessons', 'Tyler 1949: objectives, experiences, organisation, evaluation', 'Spiral curriculum (Bruner)'],
